@meta
id: 24-02
title: Move a line to the top or the bottom
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-move
par: 17
minutes: 4
vscode: cut the line, scroll, paste, scroll back, repeat
open: registry.ts

@brief
Two lines in the DI registry are in the wrong place. The `container`
import on line 5 has to be the first line of the file, because the
convention is framework imports first. And `OrderResolver` is
registered on line 10, before the providers it depends on; it has to be
the last registration in the file.

The command is `m`, move. Like `d` it takes a range in front. Unlike
`d` it also takes an argument after: the line the moved text should
land *below*. `0` means "below nothing", so the very top. `$` is the
last line, so the very bottom.

Neither move needs the cursor anywhere near the line.

@keys
| `:5m0` | move line 5 to the top of the file |
| `:10m$` | move line 10 to the bottom of the file |
| `:m0` | move the current line to the top |

@hints
Range in front, `m`, then the destination. The destination is the line
number the text goes *after*.
--
Do the import first. Moving line 5 to the top pushes lines 1 to 4 down
by one, but line 10 is still line 10, because nothing above it was added
or removed. Check the gutter if you doubt it.
--
`:5m0` and Enter. `:10m$` and Enter. `:wq`.

@solution
    :5m0<Enter>  :10m$<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes, no cursor movement, no register touched.

The argument to `m` is an address, and it means "put the lines after
this one". That is why `0` exists as an address at all: it is the only
way to say "before line 1". It comes up again with `:pu` and `:t`, where
`:0pu` and `:t0` mean the same thing.

Notice what happened to the line numbers. After the first move,
everything between the source and the destination shifted by one, so a
second command aimed at that region would have missed. Line 10 was
below both, so it did not care. When you chain several moves, work out
whether each one shifts the next one's numbers, or do the lower one
first. Chapter 15 got around this with `:g`, which marks its lines
before it moves any of them. Here you are the one keeping count.

@start registry.ts
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderResolver } from '../domain/order/order.resolver';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderResolver', { useClass: OrderResolver });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });

@expected registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderResolver } from '../domain/order/order.resolver';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
container.register('OrderResolver', { useClass: OrderResolver });
